From: XR1710G lab <lab@xr1710g.local>
Subject: [PATCH] procd: leave STATE_UBUS after a deadline instead of hanging forever

STATE_UBUS has exactly one exit: procd_state_ubus_connect(), called only
from the success branch of ubus_connect_cb().  When ubus_connect() keeps
failing, timeout_retry() just doubles the retry interval up to 1 s and
tries again, forever.  Nothing else ever advances the state machine, so
"- init -" is never reached and the board silently stops at "- ubus -".

The failure is silent by design: the log line is DEBUG(4, ...) and debug
defaults to 0, so a normal boot prints nothing at all.  Together with a
watchdog that cannot be armed, the device neither boots, reboots, nor
explains itself.

Observed on a Gemtek XR1710G (Airoha AN7581) running OpenWrt 7.3.4: the
console ends at "[13.184096] procd: - ubus -" and stays there forever.
The AN7581 wdt driver does not implement WDIOC_SETTIMEOUT, so
watchdog_init() logs "WDT failed to set timeout 30s: Invalid argument"
and leaves the timer off, which is why the hang never self-heals.  Worse,
the system never reaches STATE_INIT, so /etc/config/system is never
generated and uci reports no compat_version; fwtool.sh then falls back to
1.0 while the image metadata says 2.0, and sysupgrade refuses to run.

Give the state a deadline.  After 10 s the system moves on to STATE_INIT
with a loud error instead of hanging.  Services that need ubus fail on
their own and say so; a system that can be logged into and inspected is
strictly better than one that freezes before init.  The retry loop keeps
running in the background, so if ubusd appears later the objects get
registered and services recover.

The deadline is taken from the kernel command line, so it can be tuned
from U-Boot bootargs on a board that is already soft-bricked, without
rebuilding anything:

  procd_ubus_timeout=<seconds>   default 10, clamped to 300
  procd_ubus_timeout=0           disable the deadline (old behaviour)

The timer is cancelled as soon as the connection succeeds, so the normal
path is unchanged.  procd_state_ubus_connect() is a no-op unless the state
is still STATE_UBUS, which makes the callback safe to call unconditionally
and also keeps it inert on the reconnect path after init.

Signed-off-by: XR1710G lab <lab@xr1710g.local>
---
--- a/ubus.c
+++ b/ubus.c
@@ -18,10 +18,15 @@
 #include <signal.h>
 
 #include "procd.h"
+#include "utils/utils.h"
+
+#define UBUS_STATE_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT_MS	10000
 
 const char *ubus_socket = NULL;
 static struct ubus_context *ctx;
 static struct uloop_timeout ubus_timer;
+static struct uloop_timeout ubus_state_timer;
+static unsigned int ubus_state_timeout;
 static int timeout;
 static struct udebug_ubus udebug;
 
@@ -83,14 +88,60 @@ ubus_connect_cb(struct uloop_timeout *ti
 
 	DEBUG(2, "Connected to ubus, id=%08x\n", ctx->local_id);
 	reset_timeout();
+	uloop_timeout_cancel(&ubus_state_timer);
 	ubus_add_uloop(ctx);
 	procd_state_ubus_connect();
 }
 
+static void
+ubus_state_timeout_cb(struct uloop_timeout *t)
+{
+	/*
+	 * STATE_UBUS only leaves through procd_state_ubus_connect(), which is
+	 * called from the success path of ubus_connect_cb().  If ubusd never
+	 * answers, that success never happens and the state machine stops for
+	 * good: no init, no login, nothing.  procd_state_ubus_connect() is a
+	 * no-op unless the state is still STATE_UBUS, so it is safe to call
+	 * here unconditionally.
+	 */
+	ERROR("- ubus - no connection after %u ms, continuing without it\n",
+	       ubus_state_timeout);
+	procd_state_ubus_connect();
+}
+
+static unsigned int
+ubus_state_timeout_from_cmdline(void)
+{
+	char line[32];
+	char *v, *end;
+	unsigned long sec;
+
+	v = get_cmdline_val("procd_ubus_timeout", line, sizeof(line));
+	if (!v || *v < '0' || *v > '9')
+		return UBUS_STATE_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT_MS;
+
+	sec = strtoul(v, &end, 10);
+	if (end == v || *end != '\0')
+		return UBUS_STATE_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT_MS;
+
+	/* 0 disables the deadline, for anyone who wants the old behaviour */
+	if (sec > 300)
+		sec = 300;
+
+	return (unsigned int)(sec * 1000);
+}
+
 void
 procd_connect_ubus(void)
 {
 	ubus_timer.cb = ubus_connect_cb;
 	reset_timeout();
 	timeout_retry();
+
+	ubus_state_timeout = ubus_state_timeout_from_cmdline();
+	if (!ubus_state_timeout)
+		return;
+
+	ubus_state_timer.cb = ubus_state_timeout_cb;
+	uloop_timeout_set(&ubus_state_timer, ubus_state_timeout);
 }
